Lightningpanda patch 0024: own property names answered from the prototype; enumerable indices.

JSClassSetOwnPropertyNames gives a class the names its wrappers have as own properties while
the values stay with the prototype's accessors. Chrome has CSSOM's computed-style properties as
named properties of the object (hasOwnProperty('color') is true, Object.keys and spread list
them, getOwnPropertyDescriptor gives a data property); lightningpanda keeps them as prototype
accessors so that reads stay inline-cached. With this patch:
- getOwnSpecialPropertyNames lists the class's "listed" names (DontEnum ones only for
  getOwnPropertyNames), after what the getPropertyNames hook adds;
- getOwnPropertySlot, for an own-property lookup only (InternalMethodType::GetOwnProperty:
  hasOwnProperty, getOwnPropertyDescriptor, the copies of spread and Object.assign), answers an
  "answered" name the wrapper lacks as a writable, enumerable, configurable data property whose
  value is a [[Get]] of the name; uncacheable. A [[Get]] never reaches this (on a cacheable
  wrapper it skips getOwnPropertySlot altogether; otherwise the type check stops it), nor do
  `in` and VM inquiries.
Also: the indexed callbacks' properties (patch 0012) are enumerable, as WebIDL's are (Chrome:
writable false, enumerable and configurable true), so spread and Object.assign copy them.

